WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:06.144
A has fixed area six. Does making it narrower necessarily improve the placement?

2
00:00:06.634 --> 00:00:14.490
Early planning models some logic regions as rectangles with fixed area and adjustable shape: soft blocks.

3
00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:21.972
Fixed memory macros cannot stretch arbitrarily; only A may reshape in this example.

4
00:00:22.462 --> 00:00:27.510
A’s width and height must be positive, and their product must remain six.

5
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:32.736
Aspect ratio means width divided by height, not the reverse.

6
00:00:33.226 --> 00:00:39.018
The allowed aspect ratio is from one half to two, including both endpoints.

7
00:00:39.508 --> 00:00:47.244
The original three-by-two shape has area six and aspect ratio one point five, so it is allowed.

8
00:00:47.734 --> 00:00:55.974
Two by three also has area six; its aspect ratio is about zero point six seven, within the bounds.

9
00:00:56.464 --> 00:01:02.040
On an integer grid, first list every positive integer factor pair of six.

10
00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:09.162
One by six is too tall; six by one is too wide, so both fail the aspect-ratio limits.

11
00:01:09.652 --> 00:01:16.908
Removing those extremes leaves two by three and three by two for this integer experiment.

12
00:01:17.398 --> 00:01:25.974
A continuous model also allows noninteger dimensions; choose a positive width and calculate height as six divided by width.

13
00:01:26.464 --> 00:01:32.736
The origin is the lower-left corner; x increases rightward and y increases upward.

14
00:01:33.226 --> 00:01:41.202
A starts at the origin, three by two; D starts at zero, two, three by one, touching A’s top edge.

15
00:01:41.692 --> 00:01:50.148
The legal box is nine by three: area twenty-seven, minus block area twenty-three, leaves four empty units.

16
00:01:50.638 --> 00:01:56.838
The fixed outline is ten by eight, with area eighty; it is different from the bounding box.

17
00:01:57.328 --> 00:02:02.856
Now change only A to two by three, while leaving every other block in place.

18
00:02:03.346 --> 00:02:09.954
Will the placement remain legal after A narrows? First predict where its top edge will end.

19
00:02:12.994 --> 00:02:17.442
Watch A’s right edge retreat while its top rises from two to three.

20
00:02:17.932 --> 00:02:26.004
The new A spans x from zero to two; D spans zero to three, so their horizontal intervals overlap.

21
00:02:26.494 --> 00:02:33.462
Their shared vertical interval is two to three, giving overlap height one and overlap area two.

22
00:02:33.952 --> 00:02:41.520
When rectangles only share a boundary, an overlap dimension is zero, so touching is allowed.

23
00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:48.498
Repair the conflict first; compare bounding boxes only after confirming legality.

24
00:02:48.988 --> 00:02:53.460
Try a local repair by moving D two grid units to the right.

25
00:02:53.950 --> 00:03:00.582
D no longer covers A, but it now enters B’s space, so the repair is incomplete.

26
00:03:01.072 --> 00:03:08.088
Move D up one unit to y equals three, touching B without overlapping A or B.

27
00:03:08.578 --> 00:03:16.434
However, the top reaches four while the right edge stays nine, so bounding-box area increases to thirty-six.

28
00:03:16.924 --> 00:03:22.716
Keep A at two by three and move B left one unit until it touches A’s right edge.

29
00:03:23.206 --> 00:03:27.390
Next, move C left one unit to touch B’s right edge.

30
00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:34.272
Move D to four, two: its bottom touches C, and its left edge touches B.

31
00:03:34.762 --> 00:03:41.130
The new placement again has a nine-by-three box with area twenty-seven, matching the baseline.

32
00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:46.284
To shrink the box further, which block still determines the rightmost edge?

33
00:03:49.324 --> 00:03:54.108
First move D to zero, three, freeing the space above C.

34
00:03:54.598 --> 00:04:02.046
The box temporarily grows at this intermediate step; do not confuse an intermediate move with the final result.

35
00:04:02.536 --> 00:04:08.544
Move E to four, two, above C and right of B, without covering D.

36
00:04:09.034 --> 00:04:16.578
The rightmost edge is now six and the top is four, giving a six-by-four box with area twenty-four.

37
00:04:17.068 --> 00:04:25.452
Total block area remains twenty-three, leaving only one unoccupied unit inside this legal bounding box.

38
00:04:25.942 --> 00:04:34.302
The outline stays eighty, so fixed-outline density remains twenty-three over eighty, or twenty-eight point seven five percent.

39
00:04:34.792 --> 00:04:43.728
Packing density uses a different denominator; the baseline is twenty-three over twenty-seven, about eighty-five point two percent.

40
00:04:44.218 --> 00:04:51.978
After compaction, packing density becomes twenty-three over twenty-four, about ninety-five point eight percent.

41
00:04:52.468 --> 00:04:57.516
Whenever you report a density, state which rectangle supplies the denominator.

42
00:05:00.556 --> 00:05:07.380
Twenty-four is a verified legal witness here, not a universal result for soft-block problems.

43
00:05:07.870 --> 00:05:14.382
Consider a new block of area eight, with the same aspect-ratio bounds from one half to two.

44
00:05:14.872 --> 00:05:22.128
Do two by four and four by two pass the shape check? Calculate both area and aspect ratio.

45
00:05:25.168 --> 00:05:32.160
Both shapes are allowed: each has area eight, and their aspect ratios equal the two permitted endpoints.

46
00:05:32.650 --> 00:05:40.674
Return to the five-block lab: keep A at two by three, repair overlaps, then try reaching box area twenty-four.

47
00:05:41.164 --> 00:05:48.564
In code, check positive dimensions, area tolerance, and aspect ratio before running the placement checks.

48
00:05:49.054 --> 00:05:57.102
Keep the complete process: choose a shape, repack neighbors, check legality, and then compare the stated objective.
